## Task board

This project's work is tracked on a local Agent Board, not in your own memory. The project name on the board is `PROJECT_NAME`.

Report your progress by calling the `agent-board` CLI directly via your shell/bash tool:

```bash
agent-board list --project=PROJECT_NAME
agent-board create --title="..." --project=PROJECT_NAME --agent=claude --priority=med
agent-board update <id> --status=in_progress --agent=claude
agent-board note <id> "short note about what happened" --agent=claude
```

Always pass `--agent=claude` explicitly — don't omit it even though the flag is technically optional; leaving it off means the board can't tell who left a note or made a change.

If this project's board isn't the default one (ask whoever set up the board if unsure), add `--workspace=<name>` to every command above, e.g. `agent-board list --project=PROJECT_NAME --workspace=<name>`.

**Opening a ticket (rules):**
1. Pass `--project` exactly as the user named it (name or prefix, any case). Don't look it up in `agent-board project list` and substitute a match yourself — the board matches it and asks (exit code `2`, rule 5) when several projects fit or none does.
2. Know the project? Just run `create` — don't `list` first just to open a ticket.
3. Status: `backlog` `in_progress` `review` `done`; priority: `low` `med` `high`. Common aliases (`doing`, `wip`, `進行中`, `urgent`, `高`) are accepted and stored as the standard value.
4. Put the description in `--notes="..."`; always pass your own `--agent=`.
5. Exit code `2` / `needs your input` means the board can't tell what the user wants (several projects match, the project doesn't exist yet, no title). Ask the user that question with your built-in ask tool (AskUserQuestion / request_user_input / ask_user; none -> ask in chat), offering the printed options, then rerun with all the flags of the one they chose (including `--remember-as`, so the board learns what they meant). Never pick an option yourself, and never invent a project prefix. Don't change `agent-board config ask` unless the user tells you to.

**Splitting work (subtasks):**
- When a piece of work is split across agents or sessions, keep one parent ticket that coordinates it and give each agent its own subtask: `agent-board create --title="..." --parent=<parent id> --agent=claude` (the project comes from the parent).
- Two levels only: a subtask can't have subtasks of its own. `agent-board list` shows subtasks indented under their parent, with the parent's progress (`[2/3 done]`).
- Finishing your subtask never moves the parent. When the CLI prints `note: All N subtasks of <id> are done`, tell the user, or move the parent on if that's clearly yours to do.
- Move a subtask under another parent with `agent-board update <id> --parent=<id>`, or detach it with `--parent=none`. A ticket with subtasks can't be deleted until they're deleted or detached — ask the user before doing either.

**When a ticket changes project:** a ticket moved to another project (`agent-board move <id> --project=<name>`, only when the user asks) gets a new id there. Its old id keeps working, but when the CLI prints `note: AB-5 is now OPS-12`, use the new id from then on — in notes, commits and handoffs.

**When to call it:**
- At the start of a coding task: `list` first for an existing open ticket matching the work; if none exists, `create` one and set it `in_progress`.
- Whenever status meaningfully changes (code is ready for review, you're blocked, you're done).
- When you hit a blocker or make a non-obvious decision: `note` it.

**Important:** always call `list` fresh when you need the board's current state — never assume it still matches what you saw earlier in the conversation. Someone else (a person, or another agent) may have changed it since.
